Use the build-site skill on projects/travel-booking for the Landfall (see the place first) design in projects/travel-booking/landfall/site/: you are Developer B for round 3, building PART B of the spec (the inner pages at competitor depth: flight results, flight details and fares, travelers, checkout, confirmation, hotel results, hotel detail, guests, the account pages and dashboard; P1 items first, then P2 as time allows). FIRST wire the expanded mock catalogue (content/mock-catalogue.md) into the simulated backend: you own lib/mock/ and lib/data/ (except lib/mock/home.ts, which Developer A owns), keep every existing id and route working, and keep the export names stable so Developer A's homepage picks up the new data. Hotel photos for the bigger catalogue: build a pool of this design's own photos and assign them so no two neighbouring results repeat a photo. Developer A is building the homepage and footer in the same app at the same time; never touch its files. You own the inner page components and CSS (flights, hotels, booking, account). Read first: projects/travel-booking/logs/round3-note.txt (Varun's feedback and rulings; it binds you), logs/redesign-note.txt, logs/dev-stack-note.txt, benchmark-rubric.md, then projects/travel-booking/landfall/design/round3-spec.md (the approved round 3 spec; build YOUR PART of it) and the design's existing files (landfall/design/design-spec.md, landfall/design/tokens.css, landfall/design/choices.md, landfall/build/progress.md, landfall/build/handover.md). The competitor deconstructions in references/deconstruct/booking-com.md, expedia.md and priceline.md and the mock catalogue content/mock-catalogue.md are being finished right now; read them as soon as they exist (the catalogue is done when it ends with 'Catalogue complete.'). Never read or reuse anything from the other design (projects/travel-booking/site/, design/, prototype/, build/ and ports 3001 and 3002), except that you may read projects/travel-booking/site/public/images/credits.md only to avoid reusing one of its photos. Photos: this design's own Unsplash License photos only, each checked on its photo page and appended to public/images/credits.md; every image slot gets a real photo, never an empty box. SERVER: the Studio Lead runs one shared dev server for this app at http://localhost:3003 (navy), used by you and by the other Developer on this app at the same time; do not start another dev server and do not run production builds; if it is down, start npm run dev:navy in projects/travel-booking/landfall/site/ in the background and leave it running. Keep to your own files (listed below); shared files take small additions only, listed under your heading in projects/travel-booking/landfall/build/progress.md. QA at 1440, 1024 and 390, then ONE critic round (high effort, foreground) on your part, scored with projects/travel-booking/benchmark-rubric.md side by side against Booking.com, Expedia and Priceline, with the studio rubric score reported alongside, by 16:40 at the latest; then fix what it finds, worst first, until the hard stop. Record what you built, the scores and the fixes under your heading in projects/travel-booking/landfall/build/progress.md. Never install packages. HARD STOP 17:05. Run every subagent in the foreground, never in the background. Stop any server you start other than the shared one. Log your run in projects/travel-booking/review-log.md.